    "agent_last_message": "The tool is built and working. Run it as `node main.js --input <file> --output <file>` from `output/`; it uses only the Node standard library and has no `package.json`. All 18 tests pass, and its results matched both a simple reference implementation and a separate Python implementation on every input I tried.\n\n**Files**\n- `main.js`: the command-line entry point. It handles the arguments and exit codes.\n- `lib/analyze.js`: reads the file in chunks and does the counting.\n- `lib/word-table.js`: a compact table that counts words straight from the file's bytes.\n- `lib/report.js`: writes the JSON.\n- `test/`: run with `node --test 'test/*.test.js'`. Node 22 won't accept a bare directory there.\n\n**Decisions where the spec was silent or ambiguous** (worth checking these match what you expect):\n1. **\"Malformed\" means the file isn't valid UTF-8.** Such a file exits 1, with the byte offset of the first bad byte in the error message. Words have to come out as JSON text, so there's no faithful way to report them otherwise. One consequence: older Latin-1 encoded files will be rejected.\n2. **Lowercasing covers all languages, not just English letters**, so `CAF\u00c9` and `caf\u00e9` are the same word. I checked it agrees with Python's lowercasing for every character both know about.\n3. **Only the six listed whitespace characters separate words.** Non-breaking spaces, byte-order marks and NUL bytes count as part of a word, as the spec literally says.\n4. **Words with equal counts are ordered by Unicode code point.** JavaScript's default string order would put some emoji in the wrong place.\n5. **The JSON output is pure ASCII**, so `caf\u00e9` is written as `\"caf\\u00e9\"`. Any JSON reader turns it back into `caf\u00e9`, whatever encoding it assumes.\n6. **An output file that can't be written exits 1.** The spec doesn't list this case. Repeating a flag also exits 1, and `--input=file` is accepted as well as `--input file`.\n\n**Testing**\n- 